 "borg_goodnight_5s.txt": {
  "text": "integrated_multimodal_description: [Shot 1] r34l1sm, Live-action, cinematic, a medium close-up frames Patrick Stewart as Captain Jean-Luc Picard, a bald older man with a calm, commanding presence, wearing a red and black Starfleet uniform, seated in the captain's chair on the dim bridge of the starship Enterprise, soft amber and blue console lights glowing behind him. He holds a thick, old, gold-bound book open in his lap, its worn leather cover edged in tarnished gold, and reads aloud from it like a father reading a bedtime story: every few seconds his eyes drop to the page for a moment, then lift back up to look directly into the camera as he speaks. The framing stays a medium close-up for the whole shot, his shoulders and the top of the chair visible. The camera pans with small amplitude at very slow speed from left to right; it does not zoom or push in. The man with a deep, calm, resonant British baritone, in a measured, warm storytelling cadence (S1), says: <d>[English] Good night, Dad. Good night, Borg. Good night. Bed.</d> As the last line ends, he pauses and gives an almost imperceptible tilt of his head, only a few degrees to his left, barely noticeable, with a faint knowing smile, then holds the gaze into the camera, otherwise perfectly still, for a quiet beat until the shot ends.\n\noverall_soundscape: The low steady hum of the starship bridge, a soft electronic chirp from a console, the faint rustle of a heavy page, and the quiet creak of the captain's chair as he shifts.\n\nnon_diegetic_music: None.",
